{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-f190120a-4af3-4ef4-9a53-9885eb89e6fc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-96a4d3c284fa7833","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8010-4kgtstod/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8010-decomposition.md","content":"# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-8010\n\nParent strategy: RHAISTRAT-8010 — Operator reconciliation log standardization with structured resource context.\n\n## Triage Assessment\n\nStep 0 checks did **not** fire; full decomposition proceeded:\n\n- **Below-threshold escape — multiple distinct work streams**: although the strategy is S-sized, affects a single component (rhods-operator), and the code work is owned by a single team (ODH Operator team) with predominantly High AI-implementability mechanical conversion work, the scope spans two architecturally distinct work streams: (1) code implementation — the log-call conversion across the manager and cloudmanager binaries, and (2) substantive content authoring — the structured-log-field contribution guideline and published convention for external operator adoption (HLR-8). The guideline is a new doc topic with before/after examples and a DSCI key migration guide, not an incidental touch-up, so it warrants a separate `docs-authoring` epic with its own review cycle.\n- **Below-threshold escape — genuine unknowns**: the strategy carries an open-questions table (optional fields beyond name/namespace/resourceKind, cloudmanager release timing, audit percentage, affected alert rules). As evaluated below, none of them changes which epics exist or what they do, so no Investigation epics result — but their presence also barred the single-epic below-threshold path.\n- **Documentation-only check**: not applicable — the strategy's core is code change in rhods-operator.\n\n## Staff Engineer Input\n\nThe Staff Engineer / SME Input section was parsed first and takes precedence. The strategy's Technical Approach and HLRs already incorporate the SME corrections, so no overrides needed to be applied during decomposition:\n\n- Controller architecture is the SME-corrected **5 tiers** (DSC, 8 component controllers, module controller with 8 module handlers, DSCI, 5 service controllers), not the 4 the original draft described.\n- Action-file inventory is the SME-confirmed **15 files across 4 tiers** (8 component + 3 service + 1 DSC + 3 module) — the \"~16 component action files\" figure and the wrong-premise open question about action-file counts were already removed from the strategy body.\n- The three-problem diagnosis (message-embedded identity, non-standard DSCI keys `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization`, missing `resourceKind`), camelCase field naming, and the shared-reconciler status of cloudmanager are SME-confirmed and are used as ground truth for epic scope.\n\n## Epic List\n\n| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n| RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | Standardize manager binary reconciliation error logs with structured resource context | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8010-E002 | Standardize cloudmanager binary reconciliation error logs with structured resource context | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8010-E003 | Document and publish the structured log field convention | Implementation (docs-authoring) | ODH Operator team | P1 |\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[\"E001 — Manager binary log standardization (P0)\"]\n    E002[\"E002 — Cloudmanager binary log standardization (P0)\"]\n    E003[\"E003 — Convention documentation and publication (P1, docs-authoring)\"]\n    E001 --> E003\n    E002 --> E003\n```\n\nCritical path length: 2 (E001 → E003).\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|------|------|-----------|\n| E001 ∥ E002 (no edge) | Epic boundary rule 2 (architectural sub-system boundary) + Rule 15 | Manager and cloudmanager are two separate Go binaries in the same repo with disjoint controller sets. Same component and team, but the split follows the architectural sub-system boundary (binary), and the epics run in parallel — no shared artifacts, different areas of the repo. This split also lets cloudmanager follow in a subsequent release if PM decides so (strategy open question on release timing) without re-planning the manager work. |\n| E001 → E003 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring blocked by ALL Implementations) | The contribution guideline documents the convention as actually implemented in both binaries, so it is blocked by both implementation epics. Per the docs-authoring priority exception, this edge does not push E003's P1 upstream. |\n| E002 → E003 | Rule 11 | Same as above — the guideline must reflect the cloudmanager conversion as well. |\n\n**No standalone lint-rule epic (shared-surface enforcement deliverable).** The strategy mandates a CI lint rule / static-analysis check over the converted `log.Error` call sites (Acceptance Criteria; effort estimate item 4). Per the shared-surface enforcement rule, this deliverable is divided per surface, not by work type: each binary's conversion epic authors and verifies the part of the lint check covering its own binary's converted call sites (scope + verification AC in both E001 and E002). A standalone \"CI lint rule\" epic would be a work-type extraction.\n\n**No Investigation epics.** All strategy unknowns were evaluated against the decision rule (\"does the answer change which downstream epics exist or what they do?\"):\n\n- *Optional fields beyond name/namespace/resourceKind (PM / Observability team)* — does not change which epics exist; the required field set is fixed by the P0/P1 HLRs and the conversion proceeds identically. It affects only the guideline's content, so it is carried as an explicit open question and an acceptance criterion on E003 (the decision must be recorded — adopted or explicitly deferred).\n- *Which