cumentation content (contribution guideline + published convention), which maps to the canonical `Documentation` component. The work remains owned by the ODH Operator team per the strategy's impacted-teams table; the Cluster Observability Operator team only receives the published convention (passive — no code changes, no epic).\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n| HLR-1 — All rhods-operator reconciliation error logs embedding resource name/namespace in message strings converted to structured KV pairs (`name`, `namespace`) | P0 | E001, E002 | rhods-operator spans both binaries; manager call sites in E001, cloudmanager call sites in E002. |\n| HLR-2 — DSCI controller migrated from `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` to camelCase `name`/`namespace` | P0 | E001 | DSCI controller is in the manager binary. |\n| HLR-3 — Service controller (Gateway, Auth, Monitoring, CertConfigMapGenerator, Setup) error logs include `resourceKind` | P0 | E001 | |\n| HLR-4 — All action file error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` across 4 tiers (8 component + 3 service + 1 DSC + 3 module) | P0 | E001 | All 15 action files are manager-binary files. |\n| HLR-5 — Cloudmanager controller (AWS/Azure/CoreWeave KubernetesEngine) error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` | P0 | E002 | |\n| HLR-6 — Module controller error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` (8 module handlers) | P0 | E001 | |\n| HLR-7 — DSC and component controller error logs include `resourceKind` | P1 | E001 (bundled) — same `log.Error` call sites and controller files as the HLR-1/HLR-3 conversion pass; a separate epic would audit and edit the same files twice, and the `resourceKind` addition is one mechanical extension of the same conversion. Not separately schedulable. | Bundling recorded per Step 4. |\n| HLR-8 — Document the convention as a contribution guideline and publish for external operator adoption | P1 | E003 | docs-authoring priority exception: strategy priority Major → P1. |\n\nAll 8 HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n- **E001 size above the ~2-week guideline (managed)**: E001 is ~1.25–1.5 sprints by the strategy's effort arithmetic (audit ~0.5 sprint + the manager share of the ~0.75–1 sprint conversion). Kept whole because the conversion is one mechanical pass over the same `log.Error` call sites across the manager binary's controller tiers and action files, and a split by controller tier would audit and edit overlapping files twice while fragmenting the single lint surface for one binary. Re-split trigger: the call-site audit exceeds the strategy's ~0.5-sprint estimate (i.e., the audit finds substantially more call sites than the 75%-embedding assumption implies), pushing the epic past 2 sprints — then split by controller tier (component / service / module / DSCI) along action-file boundaries.\n- **Cloudmanager release sequencing is undecided (non-blocking)**: the strategy's open question on whether cloudmanager ships in the same release as manager or a subsequent one is a PM scheduling decision. The per-binary epic split keeps it a planning choice; it does not change either epic's scope.\n- **Optional-fields decision pending (non-blocking)**: whether the convention extends beyond `name`/`namespace`/`resourceKind` (reconciliation attempt count, error category) is owned by PM / Observability team. It gates only guideline content (E003 AC requires the decision to be recorded), not the conversion work, which implements the HLR-fixed required fields.\n- **Strategy-level process gates pending**: Architecture Review, Build Onboarding, Licence Validation, and other Prerequisites & Process Gates are marked \"Pending human review\", and the Acceptance Criteria are labelled \"Proposed — requires PM/Engineering validation\". These are launch gates on the strategy, not decomposition structure; implementing teams should confirm them before starting.\n- **Log consumer regression risk**: message strings change and DSCI key names change; existing Loki/CloudWatch queries and text-based alerts may break. Mitigated by ACs on E001/E002 (before/after ingestion verification, alert-pattern identification with SRE/Support) and by E003's DSCI migration guide with before/after query examples; single-release DSCI transition per the strategy's risk mitigation.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nNot applicable as a dependency-gated split. The only external dependency with uncertain timing is COO team adoption of the convention (Loki Operator, Tempo Operator). Per platform policy (overlay 0008) RHOAI cannot mandate or manage it, and every strategy deliverable (both binary conversions, the guideline, the publication) delivers full value without COO action — so there is no Tier 2 work to gate. COO adoption is voluntary and out of scope; E003 publishes the convention and files tracking issues in COO projects as best-effort.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":61663,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8b44e8c92a9af6ce","agent_id":"aa0e7b6d17f6c27ab","session_id":"326616fc-8f4b-4f48-9d9b-9ede56865310","uuid":"19730ad1-1a5b-43b9-9399-6814c1c72ec0","timestamp":"2026-10-26T18:33:05.076Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8010"}
