{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9be4e9a685a283d4","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8010-E003\n3\ttitle: Document and publish the structured log field convention\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8010\n5\tcomponent: Documentation\n6\tteam: ODH Operator team\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P1\n9\timplementation_type: docs-authoring\n10\tdependencies:\n11\t- RHAISTRAT-8010-E001\n12\t- RHAISTRAT-8010-E002\n13\tai_signals:\n14\t  change_specificity: 1\n15\t  pattern_precedent: 0\n16\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n17\t  existing_foundation: 0\n18\t  open_questions: -1\n19\t  external_dependency: 0\n20\t  human_process_gates: -1\n21\t  repo_access: 1\n22\t  architecture_claims: 1\n23\tinvestigation_signals: null\n24\tai_implementability: Medium\n25\tai_implementability_score: 1\n26\tjira_key: null\n27\tbranch: null\n28\tgated_by: null\n29\tgate_failure_impact: null\n30\t---\n31\t# Document and publish the structured log field convention\n32\t\n33\t## Description\n34\t\n35\tThis epic documents the rhods-operator structured log field convention as a contribution guideline for new controller development and publishes it as a specification for external operator adoption. It captures the three-problem diagnosis behind the change (message-embedded resource identity, non-standard DSCI key names, missing `resourceKind`), the required field set, the camelCase naming rationale, before/after code examples, and a DSCI key migration guide for operators. Publication targets the Cluster Observability Operator team's components (Loki Operator, Tempo Operator) on a voluntary-adoption basis per platform policy (overlay 0008: RHOAI does not install, manage, or control external operator dependencies), as confirmed in the source RFE approval comment.\n36\t\n37\t## Scope\n38\t\n39\tContent outline (authored inside the contribution guideline and published convention):\n40\t\n41\t- **Required fields**: `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` as structured key-value pairs on reconciliation error logs, including the cluster-scoped-resource rule (namespace omitted or empty).\n42\t- **camelCase naming rationale**: alignment with controller-runtime's existing structured log key conventions.\n43\t- **Three-problem diagnosis**: message-embedded identity in message strings, non-standard DSCI keys (`Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization`), and the missing `resourceKind` field — with the tier-specific reasoning (kind implied by controller name for component controllers vs. required for service/module/cloudmanager controllers).\n44\t- **Before/after code examples**: converting message-embedded identity to structured KV pairs (e.g., `log.Error(err, \"failed to reconcile Dashboard 'example' ...\")` → `log.Error(err, \"reconciliation failed\", \"name\", ..., \"namespace\", ..., \"resourceKind\", \"Dashboard\")`).\n45\t- **DSCI key migration guide**: before/after query examples for operators with existing Loki/CloudWatch queries filtering on the old `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` field names, noting the single-release transition.\n46\t- **Optional-fields decision record**: whether the convention extends beyond `name`/`namespace`/`resourceKind` (e.g., reconciliation attempt count, error category) — a decision owned by PM / the Observability team; the guideline must record it as adopted (with field specifications) or explicitly deferred with rationale (log-volume trade-off).\n47\t- **Publication for external adoption**: publish the convention and file tracking issues in the COO projects (Loki Operator, Tempo Operator); adoption remains at those teams' discretion per platform policy.\n48\t\n49\t## Acceptance Criteria\n50\t\n51\t- The contribution guideline specifies the required fields (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`), the camelCase naming rationale, and the three-problem diagnosis.\n52\t- Before/after code examples for converting message-embedded identity to structured KV pairs and a DSCI key migration guide with before/after query examples are included.\n53\t- The guideline is technically reviewed against the implemented logging changes — the documented convention matches the fields actually emitted by the converted manager and cloudmanager controllers and passes the CI lint rule in force on those binaries.\n54\t- The decision on optional fields beyond `name`/`namespace`/`resourceKind` is recorded: either the additional fields are specified, or their deferral is documented with rationale.\n55\t- The convention is published for external operator adoption and tracking issues are filed in the COO (Loki Operator, Tempo Operator) projects; adoption is voluntary and not gated by this epic.\n56\t\n57\t## HLR Traceability\n58\t\n59\t- **HLR-8 (P1)** — the structured log field convention is documented as a contribution guideline for new controller development and published for external operator adoption.\n60\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-846322d5d1dbdab9","agent_id":"aee8d03b2a70ae580","session_id":"326616fc-8f4b-4f48-9d9b-9ede56865310","uuid":"871a03e2-fdc2-432a-8282-e4b0c77b29c0","timestamp":"2026-10-26T18:35:41.356Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-9be4e9a685a283d4","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8010 decomposition"}
