{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-99b1a9b049d387e7","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-8010\n3\tscore: 14\n4\tpass: true\n5\trecommendation: accept\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: minor\n8\t  criterion: Completeness\n9\t  description: E002 does not explicitly address the strategy cluster-scoped-resource\n10\t    rule (namespace omitted or empty for cluster-scoped kinds); the architecture context\n11\t    shows the AWS/Azure/CoreWeave KubernetesEngine CRDs are Cluster-scoped, and E002\n12\t    ACs require namespace field presence for all cloudmanager controller logs without\n13\t    the qualification E001 carries. Add the cluster-scoped handling (scope bullet\n14\t    + AC) to E002 for consistency with the strategy AC and E001.\n15\terror: null\n16\t---\n17\t## Review Summary\n18\t\n19\tScore: 14/14 — pass\n20\tRecommendation: accept\n21\t\n22\tContract validator: OK (`validate_decomposition.py` exited 0 — body sections, canonical component names, Rule 11 docs-last coverage, priority inheritance, implementation-type parallelism, and shared-surface enforcement deliverables all pass). Triage consistency: `triage: null` (full decomposition) with `epic_count: 3` — no structural contradiction. Graded below is only what the validator cannot check.\n23\t\n24\t## Criterion Details\n25\t\n26\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n27\tAll 8 HLRs map to at least one epic via the traceability matrix, and each epic's HLR Traceability section lists its HLRs. P0 HLRs 1-6 are all covered (HLR-1 correctly split across E001/E002 since rhods-operator spans both binaries). HLR-7 (P1) is bundled into E001 with a `(bundled)` flag and inline rationale in the matrix row (\"same `log.Error` call sites and controller files... not separately schedulable\") — this is the compliant bundling-recording shape, not priority collapse: the resourceKind addition for DSC/component controllers is an incidental extension of the same conversion pass on the same call sites, not a distinct deferrable feature. HLR-8 (P1) maps to E003. Priority inheritance: E001/E002 are P0 with no dependencies; E003 is `docs-authoring` and derives its P1 from the strategy's Jira priority (Major → P1) per the exemption — correctly not flagged.\n28\t\n29\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n30\tFrontmatter dependencies match the summary DAG exactly: E003 depends on [E001, E002]; E001 and E002 have empty dependency lists; the diagram's arrow direction (E001 --> E003, dependency → dependent) is correct. Both edges are Rule 11 (implementations → docs-authoring) edges, which are valid and do not trigger priority inheritance. E001 ∥ E002 run parallel with no edge — correct per the architectural sub-system (binary) boundary; neither konflux-onboarding nor license-validation epics exist, so Rules 7/8/10 parallelism is not implicated. No circular dependencies, no invalid edges, no branch files. Critical path length 2 matches the S-size heuristic (1-2). Cross-check completeness: E003 consumes the implemented convention from both epics and has edges to both; the per-binary lint-rule authorship is deliberately per-surface with no data dependency requiring an edge.\n31\t\n32\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n33\tAll epics are owned by the ODH Operator team (single-team strategy — no team-boundary violation). E001/E002 split along the manager/cloudmanager binary boundary with disjoint controller sets — a valid architectural sub-system split that also preserves independent plannability of the cloudmanager release-timing open question. E001 is above the ~2-week guideline (~1.25-1.5 sprints) but is the defensible shape: a recorded estimate, a why-not-split rationale (one mechanical pass over the same call sites; a tier split would edit overlapping files twice and fragment the lint surface), and a concrete conditional re-split trigger in Health Warnings — recorded as a watch item, not docked. The shared-surface enforcement deliverable (CI lint rule) is correctly divided per surface into E001 and E002 rather than extracted into a standalone work-type epic. No scope duplication between E001 and E002 (different binaries' call sites, per-surface lint coverage). No work-product type mixing: E003 is pure docs-authoring; E001's release-notes item is incidental to the code change it documents, not a separate content deliverable.\n34\t\n35\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n36\tNo Investigation epics — and the decomposition explicitly evaluates each strategy open question against the \"does the answer change epic structure?\" decision rule, with each resolution carried into ACs, scope, or Health Warnings. Both implementation epics produce concrete artifacts (converted code, tests, lint checks); E003 produces the guideline/publication artifact with `implementation_type: docs-authoring`. All `gated_by`/`gate_failure_impact` fields are null, which is correct — no gates exist.\n37\t\n38\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n39\tAll three epics are Implementation type and carry `ai_signals` (the 9-signal rubric); none carries `investigation_signals`. E001/E002 signal values are consistent with the strategy content and rationales (mechanical conversion, in-repo precedent, existing logr foundation, no external gates). E003's lower scores are justified: `open_questions: -1` (the PM-owned optional-fields decision changes the guideline's field specification), `human_process_gates: -1` (PM decision record plus technical review in the deliverable's path), `existing_foundation: 0` (greenfield doc topic). Calibration check on `architecture_claims`: all three epics score 1 with rationales citing the actual provisioned architecture-context file (`.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/rhods-operator.md`), which does corroborate the controller-runtime/logr usage, the controller tiers, the DSC/DSCInitialization CRDs, the cloudmanager binary, and the AWS/Azure/CoreWeave KubernetesEngine CRDs — properly calibrated, not a \"no