{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-969712f19e5cad77","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8010\n3\tepic_count: 3\n4\tcritical_path_length: 2\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\t---\n9\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-8010\n10\t\n11\tParent strategy: RHAISTRAT-8010 — Operator reconciliation log standardization with structured resource context.\n12\t\n13\t## Triage Assessment\n14\t\n15\tStep 0 checks did **not** fire; full decomposition proceeded:\n16\t\n17\t- **Below-threshold escape — multiple distinct work streams**: although the strategy is S-sized, affects a single component (rhods-operator), and the code work is owned by a single team (ODH Operator team) with predominantly High AI-implementability mechanical conversion work, the scope spans two architecturally distinct work streams: (1) code implementation — the log-call conversion across the manager and cloudmanager binaries, and (2) substantive content authoring — the structured-log-field contribution guideline and published convention for external operator adoption (HLR-8). The guideline is a new doc topic with before/after examples and a DSCI key migration guide, not an incidental touch-up, so it warrants a separate `docs-authoring` epic with its own review cycle.\n18\t- **Below-threshold escape — genuine unknowns**: the strategy carries an open-questions table (optional fields beyond name/namespace/resourceKind, cloudmanager release timing, audit percentage, affected alert rules). As evaluated below, none of them changes which epics exist or what they do, so no Investigation epics result — but their presence also barred the single-epic below-threshold path.\n19\t- **Documentation-only check**: not applicable — the strategy's core is code change in rhods-operator.\n20\t\n21\t## Staff Engineer Input\n22\t\n23\tThe Staff Engineer / SME Input section was parsed first and takes precedence. The strategy's Technical Approach and HLRs already incorporate the SME corrections, so no overrides needed to be applied during decomposition:\n24\t\n25\t- Controller architecture is the SME-corrected **5 tiers** (DSC, 8 component controllers, module controller with 8 module handlers, DSCI, 5 service controllers), not the 4 the original draft described.\n26\t- Action-file inventory is the SME-confirmed **15 files across 4 tiers** (8 component + 3 service + 1 DSC + 3 module) — the \"~16 component action files\" figure and the wrong-premise open question about action-file counts were already removed from the strategy body.\n27\t- The three-problem diagnosis (message-embedded identity, non-standard DSCI keys `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization`, missing `resourceKind`), camelCase field naming, and the shared-reconciler status of cloudmanager are SME-confirmed and are used as ground truth for epic scope.\n28\t\n29\t## Epic List\n30\t\n31\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n32\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n33\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | Standardize manager binary reconciliation error logs with structured resource context | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n34\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E002 | Standardize cloudmanager binary reconciliation error logs with structured resource context | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n35\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E003 | Document and publish the structured log field convention | Implementation (docs-authoring) | ODH Operator team | P1 |\n36\t\n37\t## Dependency DAG\n38\t\n39\t```mermaid\n40\tgraph TD\n41\t    E001[\"E001 — Manager binary log standardization (P0)\"]\n42\t    E002[\"E002 — Cloudmanager binary log standardization (P0)\"]\n43\t    E003[\"E003 — Convention documentation and publication (P1, docs-authoring)\"]\n44\t    E001 --> E003\n45\t    E002 --> E003\n46\t```\n47\t\n48\tCritical path length: 2 (E001 → E003).\n49\t\n50\t## DAG Justification\n51\t\n52\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n53\t|------|------|-----------|\n54\t| E001 ∥ E002 (no edge) | Epic boundary rule 2 (architectural sub-system boundary) + Rule 15 | Manager and cloudmanager are two separate Go binaries in the same repo with disjoint controller sets. Same component and team, but the split follows the architectural sub-system boundary (binary), and the epics run in parallel — no shared artifacts, different areas of the repo. This split also lets cloudmanager follow in a subsequent release if PM decides so (strategy open question on release timing) without re-planning the manager work. |\n55\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring blocked by ALL Implementations) | The contribution guideline documents the convention as actually implemented in both binaries, so it is blocked by both implementation epics. Per the docs-authoring priority exception, this edge does not push E003's P1 upstream. |\n56\t| E002 → E003 | Rule 11 | Same as above — the guideline must reflect the cloudmanager conversion as well. |\n57\t\n58\t**No standalone lint-rule epic (shared-surface enforcement deliverable).** The strategy mandates a CI lint rule / static-analysis check over the converted `log.Error` call sites (Acceptance Criteria; effort estimate item 4). Per the shared-surface enforcement rule, this deliverable is divided per surface, not by work type: each binary's conversion epic authors and verifies the part of the lint check covering its own binary's converted call sites (scope + verification AC in both E001 and E002). A standalone \"CI lint rule\" epic would be a work-type extraction.\n59\t\n60\t**No Investigation epics.** All strategy unknowns were evaluated against the decision rule (\"does the answer change which downstream epics exist or what they do?\"):\n61\t\n62\t- *Optional fields beyond name/namespace/resourceKind (PM / Observability team)* — does not change which epics exist; the required field set is fixed by the P0/P1 HLRs and the conversion proceeds identically. It affects only the guideline's content, so it is carried as an explicit open question and an acceptance criterion on E003 (the decision must be recorded — adopted or explicitly deferred).\n63\t- *Which log-based alerts rely on message-string patterns (SRE / Support)* — this is work performed during the log-consumer regression verification, already an NFR verification method; captured as an AC on E001 and E002, not a structure-changing unknown.\n64\t- *Cloudmanager same release vs subsequent release (PM)* — a release-scheduling question; both binaries are converted either way. Handled by the per-binary epic split (independent plannability), noted in Health Warnings.\n65\t- *Actual percentage of log calls embedding identity (ODH Operator team)* — the audit is the first step of the conversion work itself; the answer changes effort, not epic structure or approach. Captured as scope (call-site inventory) in E001/E002.\n66\t\n67\t**No onboarding chain.** rhods-operator is an existing platform component with an architecture-context file and existing Konflux build (`Dockerfiles/Dockerfile.konflux`), so no `repo-onboarding`, `konflux-onboarding`, or `license-validation` epics are generated.\n68\t\n69\t**No upstream/external epic for COO.** Per platform policy (overlay 0008), RHOAI does not install, manage, or control external operator dependencies; the RFE approval comment confirms publishing the convention for voluntary adoption. Publishing and filing tracking issues in COO projects is scope on E003; COO adoption itself is out of scope (see Tiered Delivery).\n70\t\n71\t## Component Mapping\n72\t\n73\t- **E001 / E002 — `OpenShift AI Productization`**: the strategy's affected component `rhods-operator` (manager + cloudmanager binaries, downstream of opendatahub-io/opendatahub-operator) has no verbatim entry in `.context/rhai-components.txt`; the closest canonical RHAI Jira component for RHOAI operator productization work is `OpenShift AI Productization`. Non-obvious mapping logged here per the component-name constraint. The architecture-context file `architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/rhods-operator.md` corroborates the component's surfaces used by both epics.\n74\t- **E003 — `Documentation`**: the epic's deliverable is documentation content (contribution guideline + published convention), which maps to the canonical `Documentation` component. The work remains owned by the ODH Operator team per the strategy's impacted-teams table; the Cluster Observability Operator team only receives the published convention (passive — no code changes, no epic).\n75\t\n76\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n77\t\n78\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n79\t|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n80\t| HLR-1 — All rhods-operator reconciliation error logs embedding resource name/namespace in message strings converted to structured KV pairs (`name`, `namespace`) | P0 | E001, E002 | rhods-operator spans both binaries; manager call sites in E001, cloudmanager call sites in E002. |\n81\t| HLR-2 — DSCI controller migrated from `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` to camelCase `name`/`namespace` | P0 | E001 | DSCI controller is in the manager binary. |\n82\t| HLR-3 — Service controller (Gateway, Auth, Monitoring, CertConfigMapGenerator, Setup) error logs include `resourceKind` | P0 | E001 | |\n83\t| HLR-4 — All action file error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` across 4 tiers (8 component + 3 service + 1 DSC + 3 module) | P0 | E001 | All 15 action files are manager-binary files. |\n84\t| HLR-5 — Cloudmanager controller (AWS/Azure/CoreWeave KubernetesEngine) error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` | P0 | E002 | |\n85\t| HLR-6 — Module controller error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` (8 module handlers) | P0 | E001 | |\n86\t| HLR-7 — DSC and component controller error logs include `resourceKind` | P1 | E001 (bundled) — same `log.Error` call sites and controller files as the HLR-1/HLR-3 conversion pass; a separate epic would audit and edit the same files twice, and the `resourceKind` addition is one mechanical extension of the same conversion. Not separately schedulable. | Bundling recorded per Step 4. |\n87\t| HLR-8 — Document the convention as a contribution guideline and publish for external operator adoption | P1 | E003 | docs-authoring priority exception: strategy priority Major → P1. |\n88\t\n89\tAll 8 HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n90\t\n91\t## Health Warnings\n92\t\n93\t- **E001 size above the ~2-week guideline (managed)**: E001 is ~1.25–1.5 sprints by the strategy's effort arithmetic (audit ~0.5 sprint + the manager share of the ~0.75–1 sprint conversion). Kept whole because the conversion is one mechanical pass over the same `log.Error` call sites across the manager binary's controller tiers and action files, and a split by controller tier would audit and edit overlapping files twice while fragmenting the single lint surface for one binary. Re-split trigger: the call-site audit exceeds the strategy's ~0.5-sprint estimate (i.e., the audit finds substantially more call sites than the 75%-embedding assumption implies), pushing the epic past 2 sprints — then split by controller tier (component / service / module / DSCI) along action-file boundaries.\n94\t- **Cloudmanager release sequencing is undecided (non-blocking)**: the strategy's open question on whether cloudmanager ships in the same release as manager or a subsequent one is a PM scheduling decision. The per-binary epic split keeps it a planning choice; it does not change either epic's scope.\n95\t- **Optional-fields decision pending (non-blocking)**: whether the convention extends beyond `name`/`namespace`/`resourceKind` (reconciliation attempt count, error category) is owned by PM / Observability team. It gates only guideline content (E003 AC requires the decision to be recorded), not the conversion work, which implements the HLR-fixed required fields.\n96\t- **Strategy-level process gates pending**: Architecture Review, Build Onboarding, Licence Validation, and other Prerequisites & Process Gates are marked \"Pending human review\", and the Acceptance Criteria are labelled \"Proposed — requires PM/Engineering validation\". These are launch gates on the strategy, not decomposition structure; implementing teams should confirm them before starting.\n97\t- **Log consumer regression risk**: message strings change and DSCI key names change; existing Loki/CloudWatch queries and text-based alerts may break. Mitigated by ACs on E001/E002 (before/after ingestion verification, alert-pattern identification with SRE/Support) and by E003's DSCI migration guide with before/after query examples; single-release DSCI transition per the strategy's risk mitigation.\n98\t\n99\t## Tiered Delivery\n100\t\n101\tNot applicable as a dependency-gated split. The only external dependency with uncertain timing is COO team adoption of the convention (Loki Operator, Tempo Operator). Per platform policy (overlay 0008) RHOAI cannot mandate or manage it, and every strategy deliverable (both binary conversions, the guideline, the publication) delivers full value without COO action — so there is no Tier 2 work to gate. COO adoption is voluntary and out of scope; E003 publishes the convention and files tracking issues in COO projects as best-effort.\n102\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9f61a197868ca609","agent_id":"a6fb2e371bd48465e","session_id":"326616fc-8f4b-4f48-9d9b-9ede56865310","uuid":"f6a3672f-0fa0-4d36-9ff9-fdd4138d0c83","timestamp":"2026-10-26T18:38:45.667Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-969712f19e5cad77","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-8010 decomposition"}
